---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What’s the most important item?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "DNSSEC: it makes all forged answers detectable. Registrar lock and 2FA are equally critical for preventing hijacking."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How often should I review?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Quarterly for a full audit, and after any infrastructure or provider change."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Does DNS threat protection affect performance?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "DNSSEC adds ~100-200 bytes per response; anycast DNS absorbs attacks at the edge. Impact is negligible."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What’s the first step?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Run the homepage security scanner to see your current posture, then work through this list."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Is my DNS provider secure?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Major providers (Cloudflare, Route 53, Google Cloud DNS) have strong internal controls, but your registrar account is the soft target."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Can DNS attacks affect my email?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yes: DNS hijacking or poisoning can redirect MX records, intercepting all your mail. Email authentication (SPF/DKIM/DMARC) is a defense."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="DNS Threat Protection Checklist | PromptDNS"
  description="dns threat protection checklist"
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">DNS Threat Protection Checklist</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DNS Threat Protection Checklist</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">A complete checklist to harden your DNS against hijacking, poisoning, tunneling, and amplification attacks.</p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">The checklist</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li class="leading-relaxed">Enable DNSSEC at your DNS host and publish the DS record at your registrar.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Enable registrar lock and two-factor authentication on your registrar account.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Set CAA records to restrict certificate issuance to your trusted CAs.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Disable open recursion if you self-host DNS.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Monitor DNS record changes with automated alerts.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Use a managed DNS provider with anycast network and DDoS protection.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Keep nameserver software updated if self-hosting.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Restrict zone transfers (AXFR) to known secondary IPs only.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Set a reasonable SOA minimum TTL for negative caching.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Monitor Certificate Transparency logs for unauthorized certificates.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What’s the most important item?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">DNSSEC: it makes all forged answers detectable. Registrar lock and 2FA are equally critical for preventing hijacking.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How often should I review?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Quarterly for a full audit, and after any infrastructure or provider change.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does DNS threat protection affect performance?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">DNSSEC adds ~100-200 bytes per response; anycast DNS absorbs attacks at the edge. Impact is negligible.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What’s the first step?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Run the homepage security scanner to see your current posture, then work through this list.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is my DNS provider secure?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Major providers (Cloudflare, Route 53, Google Cloud DNS) have strong internal controls, but your registrar account is the soft target.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can DNS attacks affect my email?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: DNS hijacking or poisoning can redirect MX records, intercepting all your mail. Email authentication (SPF/DKIM/DMARC) is a defense.</p></details></div>
      <div class="mt-10 flex items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 px-5 py-3">
        <span class="grid h-9 w-9 place-items-center rounded-full bg-gradient-to-br from-accent to-violet font-display text-xs font-bold text-canvas">PD</span>
        <div><p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">PromptDNS Team</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint">Last updated: September 2026</p></div>
      </div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/dns-security/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DNS security hub →</a></li><li><a href="/what-is-dnssec/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">What is DNSSEC →</a></li><li><a href="/dns-hijacking/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DNS hijacking →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
